3. Future Memory Value and Optimal Admission

Given current store \mathcal{S} and candidate m, the decision is between two continuations:

· Admit: receive immediate net value -c(m) and then continue from store \mathcal{S}\cup\{m\}.
· Reject: continue from store \mathcal{S}.

Because V already encodes the optimal future behaviour, the optimal action is determined by the difference in total expected return. Define the Future Memory Value:

\boxed{ \operatorname{FMV}(m;\mathcal{S}) \;=\; V(\mathcal{S}\cup\{m\}) - V(\mathcal{S}) - c(m) } \tag{1}

The Bellman optimality equation for the pre‑admission state (\mathcal{S}, m) reads

V_{\text{pre}}(\mathcal{S}, m) = \max_{a\in\{0,1\}} \bigl( -c(m)\cdot a + V(\mathcal{S}_a) \bigr),

where \mathcal{S}_0 = \mathcal{S}, \mathcal{S}_1 = \mathcal{S}\cup\{m\}. Hence the optimal admission policy is

a^* = \begin{cases}
1 & \text{if } \operatorname{FMV}(m;\mathcal{S}) > 0,\\
0 & \text{otherwise}.
\end{cases}

This is an immediate consequence of the definition of the optimal value function; we view it as the Memory Admission Optimality Principle. The subsequent sections are devoted to establishing the properties of FMV, constructing adaptive mechanisms, and providing learning guarantees – the non‑trivial parts of the theory.

