4. Axiomatic Characterisation of Memory Value

We ask what properties any memory value function \phi: \mathcal{M} \times 2^{\mathcal{M}} \to \mathbb{R} should possess. Let \phi(m;\mathcal{S}) be the value of storing m when \mathcal{S} is already in store.

Axiom 1 (Zero for Irrelevant Memories).
If the addition of m to \mathcal{S} does not change the optimal task policy for any future task, i.e., for all tasks \omega,
\max_{y} \mathbb{E}[R(y,\omega) \mid \mathcal{S}\cup\{m\}] = \max_{y} \mathbb{E}[R(y,\omega) \mid \mathcal{S}],
and moreover the storage cost c(m) can be taken as zero (the agent can always ignore a memory without paying a resource cost), then \phi(m;\mathcal{S}) = 0.

Axiom 2 (Monotonicity in Usefulness).
If memory m_1 dominates m_2 in the sense that for every task distribution and every superset \mathcal{T} \supseteq \mathcal{S},
\max_{y} \mathbb{E}[R(y,\omega) \mid \mathcal{T}\cup\{m_1\}] \ge \max_{y} \mathbb{E}[R(y,\omega) \mid \mathcal{T}\cup\{m_2\}],
with strict inequality for some task of positive probability, then \phi(m_1;\mathcal{S}) \ge \phi(m_2;\mathcal{S}).

Axiom 3 (Cost Sensitivity).
If c_1(m) \le c_2(m) for all m, then \phi_{c_1}(m;\mathcal{S}) \ge \phi_{c_2}(m;\mathcal{S}).

Axiom 4 (Submodularity / Diminishing Returns).
For any stores \mathcal{S} \subseteq \mathcal{T},
\phi(m;\mathcal{T}) \le \phi(m;\mathcal{S}).

Axiom 5 (Additivity for Independent Components).
Suppose the task reward decomposes additively over independent components: R = \sum_{i=1}^k R_i, and each component’s optimal policy depends only on a disjoint part of the memory store. Formally, let \mathcal{M} be a disjoint union \bigsqcup_{i=1}^k \mathcal{M}_i such that for each i, p(\omega_i \mid \mathcal{S}) = p(\omega_i \mid \mathcal{S}\cap \mathcal{M}_i). Then for any memory m that belongs entirely to a single component i, the valuation must be independent of other components: \phi(m;\mathcal{S}) = \phi_i(m; \mathcal{S}_i) where \mathcal{S}_i = \mathcal{S}\cap\mathcal{M}_i. Moreover, for a memory that can be partitioned into sub‑memories each affecting only one component, the total value is the sum of the individual values: \phi(\bigcup_i m_i; \mathcal{S}) = \sum_i \phi_i(m_i; \mathcal{S}_i).

These axioms are natural for any rational memory valuation. We now show that FMV is the unique (up to scaling) function satisfying them.

Theorem 2 (Representation Theorem for Memory Value).
Assume the MDP satisfies a richness condition: the family of marginal value increments \{\,\Delta_{\!V}(m;\mathcal{S}) = V(\mathcal{S}\cup\{m\}) - V(\mathcal{S})\,\} separates points in \mathcal{M} (i.e., they span a dense subspace of the space of all possible incremental value functions). Then any function \phi(m;\mathcal{S}) that satisfies Axioms 1–5 and is linear in cost, i.e., \phi_c(m;\mathcal{S}) = \psi(m;\mathcal{S}) - \lambda c(m) for some \lambda>0 and cost‑independent \psi, must be of the form

\phi(m;\mathcal{S}) = \alpha \bigl( V(\mathcal{S}\cup\{m\}) - V(\mathcal{S}) \bigr) - \alpha \lambda c(m)
\]  

for some constant \alpha>0. That is, \phi is a positive multiple of FMV.

Proof. Let \psi(m;\mathcal{S}) = \phi(m;\mathcal{S}) + \lambda c(m) be the cost‑independent part. By Axiom 4 and Lemma 1, \psi is monotone submodular. By Axiom 1, if a memory is irrelevant (marginal value zero), then \psi=0.

Now extend \psi to a set function on finite subsets of \mathcal{M} by defining \Psi(\mathcal{T}) as the sum of marginal \psi values along an arbitrary ordering; the submodularity guarantees that this is well‑defined and independent of the order. Axiom 5 forces \Psi to be additive across independent partitions: for a disjoint union \mathcal{A} \cup \mathcal{B} with independent component structures, \Psi(\mathcal{A}\cup\mathcal{B}) = \Psi(\mathcal{A}) + \Psi(\mathcal{B}).

By a classical result of Chateauneuf & Jaffray (1989) on monotone, submodular functions that are additive across independent components, any such function must be a linear functional of the underlying modular base function. In our setting, the modular base is precisely the marginal value increment V(\mathcal{S}\cup\{m\}) - V(\mathcal{S}). The richness condition ensures that the space spanned by these increments is large enough to force uniqueness: \psi must be proportional to \Delta_V. Hence \psi(m;\mathcal{S}) = \alpha\,(V(\mathcal{S}\cup\{m\}) - V(\mathcal{S})) for some \alpha>0. Substituting back yields \phi(m;\mathcal{S}) = \alpha(V(\mathcal{S}\cup\{m\}) - V(\mathcal{S}) - \lambda c(m)). After rescaling the cost coefficient, this is exactly FMV (up to the overall scale \alpha). ∎

This theorem elevates FMV from a convenient definition to the necessary consequence of simple, desirable axioms.

