5. Impossibility of Static Thresholds

Theorem 3 (No Universal Static Threshold).
There exist two memory‑augmented MDPs \mathcal{E}_1, \mathcal{E}_2 (differing only in the distribution of memory candidates or task rewards) such that the unique optimal admission policies require thresholds \tau_1 \neq \tau_2. Consequently, no fixed \tau can be optimal for all environments.

Proof. Let the storage cost be constant c(m)\equiv c_0. In \mathcal{E}_1 all candidate memories have value increments \Delta V drawn from a distribution concentrated on v_H with v_H - c_0 > 0; the optimal policy admits all candidates. In \mathcal{E}_2 the increments are concentrated on v_L with v_L - c_0 < 0; the optimal policy rejects all candidates. A static threshold \tau would either reject some valuable memories in \mathcal{E}_1 (if \tau > v_H - c_0) or admit worthless ones in \mathcal{E}_2 (if \tau \le v_L - c_0). No single threshold works for both. ∎

The impossibility extends to non‑stationary environments within a single lifetime: a threshold that is optimal early in training may become suboptimal as the agent’s policy improves. This simple result motivates an adaptive threshold that responds to the statistics of the incoming memory stream.

