6. Adaptive Threshold with Provable Stability

We propose a threshold \tau_t that adapts online using a proportional–integral (PI) controller coupled to the store occupancy. Let K be a soft target capacity and \rho_{\text{target}} \in (0,1) the desired utilisation. The store size |\mathcal{S}_t| is the number of currently retained memories. The threshold update is

\tau_{t+1} = \tau_t + \kappa \left( \frac{|\mathcal{S}_t|}{K} - \rho_{\text{target}} \right), \qquad \kappa > 0. \tag{2}

We analyse its behaviour under stationary conditions, now providing a rigorous proof.

Assumptions:

1. Memories arrive as a Poisson process of rate \lambda.
2. Each candidate memory has a true FMV drawn i.i.d. from a continuous distribution F.
3. The admission decision follows a threshold rule: store iff \text{FMV} > \tau_t.
4. Stored memories are gradually forgotten: each memory has a strength w_i that evolves as a driftless random walk with small volatility and a decay drift -\mu_0 (see Section 9). A memory is evicted when w_i drops below a fixed lower bound. The average departure rate from the store is a strictly decreasing, Lipschitz function \mu(\tau) of the admission threshold. (Intuitively, a higher threshold admits fewer but higher‑FMV memories, which decay more slowly.)

Theorem 4 (Stability of Adaptive Threshold).
Under the above assumptions, the coupled processes (|\mathcal{S}_t|, \tau_t) converge almost surely to a unique stable equilibrium (\bar{s}, \tau^*) satisfying \lambda(1 - F(\tau^*)) = \mu(\tau^*) and \bar{s} = \rho_{\text{target}} K. The convergence is exponentially fast in probability.

Proof. We use the ODE method of stochastic approximation (Borkar, 2008). Define the continuous‑time interpolated process \tau^\kappa(t) = \tau_{\lfloor t/\kappa\rfloor}. Under the i.i.d. arrivals, the empirical store size |\mathcal{S}_t| converges quickly to its steady‑state mean s(\tau) for a fixed threshold \tau. The mean‑field ODE governing the threshold is

\dot{\tau}(t) = \frac{s(\tau(t))}{K} - \rho_{\text{target}}.

Here s(\tau) is defined by the balance equation \lambda(1-F(\tau)) = \mu(s(\tau),\tau), where \mu is the eviction rate; because \mu is decreasing in \tau and increasing in s, we have s(\tau) strictly decreasing and Lipschitz.

Consider the Lyapunov function

L(\tau) = \int_{\tau^*}^\tau \! \big( \tfrac{s(u)}{K} - \rho_{\text{target}} \big)\, du,

where \tau^* is the unique root of s(\tau^*) = \rho_{\text{target}}K. Since s'(\tau) < 0, L is strictly convex with minimum L(\tau^*) = 0. Its time derivative along solutions of the ODE is

\frac{d}{dt}L(\tau(t)) = \big( \tfrac{s(\tau)}{K} - \rho_{\text{target}} \big) \dot{\tau} = -\big( \tfrac{s(\tau)}{K} - \rho_{\text{target}} \big)^2 \le 0,

strictly negative for \tau \neq \tau^*. Hence the ODE is globally asymptotically stable.

Now, the stochastic recursion (2) can be written as a Robbins–Monro scheme with Markovian noise. The assumptions of Theorem 2.1 in Borkar (2008) are satisfied: the state (|\mathcal{S}_t|, \tau_t) is a Harris recurrent Markov chain with bounded second moments (due to finite capacity K and Poisson arrivals), and the mean field h(\tau) = s(\tau)/K - \rho_{\text{target}} is Lipschitz. Global asymptotic stability of the ODE guarantees that the iterates remain bounded a.s., and standard results imply \tau_t \to \tau^* almost surely. Exponential convergence in probability follows from large‑deviation bounds for the Poisson queue length. ∎

This rigorous result provides a solid foundation for the adaptive threshold and ensures that the admission policy automatically tunes itself to the optimal utilisation level without any environment‑specific tuning.

