7. Information‑Theoretic Bound

We now relate FMV to the value of information. Suppose the task latent variable Z is drawn from a prior p(z), and the store \mathcal{S} induces a posterior p(z \mid \mathcal{S}). The optimal task action yields expected reward

U(\mathcal{S}) = \mathbb{E}_{z \sim p(\cdot\mid\mathcal{S})}\bigl[ \max_y \mathbb{E}[R(y,Z) \mid z] \bigr].

Lemma 2 (Pinsker‑Type Bound).
If the reward is bounded in [0, R_{\max}], then for any memory m,

U(\mathcal{S}\cup\{m\}) - U(\mathcal{S}) \le \sqrt{ \tfrac12 R_{\max}^2 \, D_{\mathrm{KL}}\bigl( p(z\mid\mathcal{S}\cup\{m\}) \,\|\, p(z\mid\mathcal{S}) \bigr) }.

Proof. By Pinsker’s inequality, the total variation distance between the two posteriors satisfies \delta_{\mathrm{TV}} \le \sqrt{\frac12 D_{\mathrm{KL}}}. The difference in expected maxima of functions bounded by R_{\max} is at most R_{\max} \delta_{\mathrm{TV}}. ∎

To obtain a bound on the value increment \Delta V_{\mathcal{S}}(m) that appears in FMV, we must sum over future tasks. For simplicity, assume the agent faces a sequence of i.i.d. tasks and the memory m influences the posterior for all future steps. Then the discounted sum yields

\Delta V_{\mathcal{S}}(m) \le \frac{R_{\max}}{1-\gamma} \sqrt{ \tfrac12 I(m; Z \mid \mathcal{S}) },
\]  

where I(m; Z \mid \mathcal{S}) is the conditional mutual information between the memory and the task latent variable given the current store.

In practice, the influence of a single memory may decay as subsequent memories are added. The bound can be tightened by considering the effective information horizon—the period over which m remains the most relevant piece of information—or by using the discounted sum of conditional mutual information along trajectories. Nevertheless, the above inequality provides a principled, non‑vacuous upper bound: a memory’s value is at most the square root of the information it provides about the task latent variable. Moreover, when the reward function is locally quadratic (e.g., mean‑squared error) and the posteriors are close in a Gaussian approximation, the bound becomes tight up to constants, because U(\mathcal{S}\cup\{m\}) - U(\mathcal{S}) \approx \frac12 R'' \chi^2 \approx D_{\mathrm{KL}}. This observation directly justifies the hybrid intrinsic reward used in CMI‑Mem (2024), where conditional mutual information is added to the task reward as a surrogate for memory value.

