8. Learning FMV from Experience

8.1 TD‑Error as an FMV Estimator

In practice, the true value function V is unknown. We approximate it with a learned critic \hat{V}(\mathcal{S}; \phi). The difference

\hat{\Delta}(\mathcal{S}, m) = \hat{V}(\mathcal{S}\cup\{m\}; \phi) - \hat{V}(\mathcal{S}; \phi)
\]  

provides a sample of the value increment. A natural FMV estimate is \widehat{\mathrm{FMV}} = \hat{\Delta} - c(m).

Theorem 5 (TD‑Error‑Based FMV Suboptimality Bound).
Suppose the critic \hat{V} satisfies \|V^* - \hat{V}\|_\infty \le \epsilon, where V^* is the true optimal value function, and the critic has been trained under a fixed admission policy that guarantees this accuracy. Then the admission policy \hat{a} = \mathbf{1}[\widehat{\mathrm{FMV}} > 0] achieves cumulative discounted reward that differs from the optimal by at most \frac{2\epsilon}{1-\gamma}.

Proof. The Q‑function for the admission action is \hat{Q}(\mathcal{S},m,1) = -c(m) + \hat{V}(\mathcal{S}\cup\{m\}) and \hat{Q}(\mathcal{S},m,0) = \hat{V}(\mathcal{S}). The error is |\hat{Q} - Q^*| \le \epsilon uniformly. Standard policy deviation bounds (Bertsekas & Tsitsiklis, 1996, Prop. 6.1) give the result. ∎

If the critic is trained online with a stable admission policy (e.g., the PI‑controlled threshold from Theorem 4), the visitation distribution is ergodic, and fitted Q‑iteration with a sufficiently rich function class can achieve \epsilon \to 0 as training data increases.

8.2 Meta‑Learning of Admission Parameters

We parametrise the admission policy as a stochastic rule, e.g.,

\pi_\theta(a=1 \mid m, \mathcal{S}) = \sigma\!\left( \frac{\widehat{\mathrm{FMV}}_\theta(m;\mathcal{S}) - \tau_\theta}{T} \right),
\]  

where \theta collects the parameters of the FMV estimator and threshold, and T>0 is a temperature. The agent learns \theta by following the policy gradient of the average return J(\theta) = \mathbb{E}[\sum_t \gamma^t r_t]. Using a baseline b(\mathcal{S}) to reduce variance, the update is

\theta_{t+1} = \theta_t + \beta_t \, \nabla_\theta \log \pi_{\theta_t}(a_t \mid m_t, \mathcal{S}_t) \, \bigl( Q^{\pi_{\theta_t}}(\mathcal{S}_t, m_t, a_t) - b(\mathcal{S}_t) \bigr).

Theorem 6 (Convergence of Meta‑Learning).
Assume the process (\mathcal{S}_t, m_t) is positive Harris recurrent under the adaptive threshold scheme, the critic uses linear function approximation with compatible features (as defined in Konda & Tsitsiklis, 2003), and the step sizes satisfy \sum \beta_t = \infty, \sum \beta_t^2 < \infty, with the critic updated on a faster timescale (\alpha_t / \beta_t \to 0). Then \theta_t converges almost surely to a locally optimal parameter, i.e., a stationary point of J(\theta).

Proof sketch. The proof follows from the two‑timescale actor–critic convergence theorem of Konda & Tsitsiklis (2003). The key conditions are: (i) the Markov chain induced by the policy is ergodic; (ii) the critic’s linear features are compatible with the policy gradient; (iii) the faster critic timescale allows it to track the true value function of the current policy.

Ergodicity is guaranteed by the stability of the PI controller (Theorem 4) and the soft threshold policy, which ensure that the store size remains bounded and the state distribution mixes. Note that here the threshold \tau_\theta is itself part of the policy parameters and evolves slowly; because the PI controller (2) can be seen as an outer loop that adapts on a timescale even slower than the actor, the mean‑field ODE analysis remains valid for each fixed \theta during critic convergence. Thus the process is Harris recurrent. The remaining conditions are standard, and the theorem of Konda & Tsitsiklis applies directly, yielding almost‑sure convergence to a local optimum of J(\theta). ∎

