9. Cost Model and Graceful Forgetting

We model storage cost as

c(m) = \alpha \cdot \dim(m) + \beta \cdot \log(|\mathcal{S}|+1),
\]  

reflecting embedding size and retrieval overhead. To avoid hard forgetting cliffs, each stored memory i maintains a strength w_i \in \mathbb{R} that evolves via

w_i(t+1) = w_i(t) + \eta \bigl( \widehat{\mathrm{FMV}}_i(t) - \tau_t \bigr) + \varepsilon_t, \quad \varepsilon_t \sim \mathcal{N}(0,\sigma^2),
\]  

where \widehat{\mathrm{FMV}}_i is periodically re‑estimated (e.g., after each episode or at fixed intervals). A memory is evicted when w_i < w_{\min}.

Theorem 7 (Sigmoidal Forgetting Curve).
Assume that the threshold \tau_t \approx \tau^* is approximately constant over the forgetting timescale, and that the FMV estimates are unbiased with constant mean \mu_{\mathrm{FMV}}. Moreover, suppose re‑estimation occurs on a timescale much slower than the decay of memory strengths, so that the drift d = \eta(\mu_{\mathrm{FMV}} - \tau^*) remains effectively constant between successive re‑evaluations. Then, over an interval of length t, the probability that a memory is still retained is approximately

P(\text{retained}) \approx \Phi\!\left( \frac{w_0 + d\, t - w_{\min}}{\sigma \sqrt{t}} \right) - \exp\!\left( -\frac{2d(w_0 - w_{\min})}{\sigma^2} \right) \Phi\!\left( \frac{-w_0 - d\, t + w_{\min}}{\sigma \sqrt{t}} \right),
\]  

where \Phi is the standard normal CDF. For d>0 and large t, the second term is negligible, yielding a sigmoidal shape proportional to \Phi\big((w_0 + d t - w_{\min})/\sigma\sqrt{t}\big). This avoids the abrupt memory loss (“critical age”) characteristic of simple fixed‑age or fixed‑size eviction policies.

Proof. The strength update, under the stated assumptions, is a Gaussian random walk with drift d. The first passage time to the absorbing boundary w_{\min} follows an inverse Gaussian distribution, whose survival function is exactly the expression above. For d>0 (the memory is predicted to be valuable), the drift is positive, and the survival probability initially increases, then eventually decays sigmoidally, as the diffusion term dominates. If re‑estimation updates the drift, the overall forgetting curve becomes a concatenation of such sigmoidal segments, maintaining smoothness. ∎

This consolidation mechanism naturally combines write‑time and retrieval‑time signals, as the FMV can be re‑estimated whenever the memory is accessed, consistent with the Memory‑Worth paradigm (2025).

