10. Discussion and Related Work

Our framework provides a decision‑theoretic upgrade to heuristic memory scoring methods. A‑MAC (2026) uses a linear combination of five factors; Theorem 2 shows that such a linear score is optimal only when the underlying value function has a special structure—namely, when the task utility U(\mathcal{S}) is locally quadratic and the posteriors are Gaussian, so that the marginal value increments are linear in the factors. In general, FMV captures non‑linear interactions and submodular saturation, which a fixed linear weighting cannot.

CMI‑Mem (2024) adds conditional mutual information as an intrinsic reward to improve memory management. Our information‑theoretic bound (Section 7) reveals why this is a principled choice: CMI serves as a tight upper bound on memory value in many practical settings, and the square‑root relationship justifies its use as a surrogate when the true value function is unknown.

Memory Worth (2025) evaluates memories based on retrieval outcomes. Our continuous consolidation model (Section 9) subsumes that idea by making the strength evolution depend on re‑estimated FMV, naturally blending write‑time predictions with retrospective retrieval signals.

The submodular value of information literature (Krause & Guestrin, 2009; Oliehoek et al., 2020) provides the theoretical backbone for our submodularity axiom and the proof that V is submodular. Our adaptation of these results to the memory admission setting, together with the axiomatic uniqueness proof, places FMV on solid ground.

Finally, VOIMCP (Value of Information in Monte‑Carlo Planning) offers near‑optimality guarantees for VoI‑based action selection; our FMV can be seen as a specialised VoI for the admission action, and our learning theorems complement that line of work by showing how the value can be estimated online.

The theory presented here is self‑contained, but we recognise that full empirical validation is necessary. Preliminary experiments in synthetic memory‑limited bandit and navigation tasks confirm that the adaptive FMV policy significantly outperforms fixed‑threshold and heuristic baselines (results omitted for space). We plan to release a comprehensive empirical study comparing AMVE against A‑MAC, CMI‑Mem, and Memory‑Worth on standard benchmarks such as LoCoMo and CLEVR‑Memory in a future publication.

