11. Conclusion

We have provided a self‑contained, fully proved theory of memory admission. The Future Memory Value emerges as the unique decision‑theoretic quantity satisfying natural axioms. Adaptive thresholding, information‑theoretic grounding, and reinforcement‑learning integration are all supported by rigorous theorems. This work transforms memory management from heuristic engineering into a principled optimal control problem. The immediate next steps involve deploying the adaptive PI controller in large‑scale language agents and extending the framework to distributed, multi‑agent memory stores—where the submodularity and additivity properties may offer new decentralized coordination algorithms.

Appendix A. Proofs for Sections 4–5

A.1 Submodularity of V (Lemma 1)

Let the memory‑augmented MDP be as defined in Section 2. The immediate task reward for a given store \mathcal{S} and task latent variable \omega is

r(\mathcal{S}, \omega) = \max_{y \in \mathcal{Y}} \mathbb{E}[R(y, \omega) \mid \mathcal{S}].

Assumption S1 (Submodular immediate reward). For every \omega, the set function \mathcal{S} \mapsto r(\mathcal{S}, \omega) is monotone non‑decreasing and submodular, i.e., for all \mathcal{S} \subseteq \mathcal{T} and m \notin \mathcal{T},

r(\mathcal{S} \cup \{m\}, \omega) - r(\mathcal{S}, \omega) \;\ge\; r(\mathcal{T} \cup \{m\}, \omega) - r(\mathcal{T}, \omega).

Additionally, future memory arrivals and task latent variables are conditionally independent of the current store given the history up to the current task. This ensures that the Bellman operator for the admission MDP acts on the value function only through the max over admission actions and the expected immediate reward.

Let \mathcal{V} be the space of bounded, monotone, submodular functions V : 2^{\mathcal{M}} \to \mathbb{R}, where 2^{\mathcal{M}} is the family of finite subsets of \mathcal{M}. The Bellman operator \mathcal{T} for the memory‑augmented MDP is

(\mathcal{T}V)(\mathcal{S}) = \mathbb{E}_{m \sim P(\cdot \mid h)}\!\Big[ \max\!\big( V(\mathcal{S}),\, V(\mathcal{S} \cup \{m\}) - c(m) \big) \Big] + \gamma \,\mathbb{E}_{\omega}\big[ r(\mathcal{S}, \omega) \big],

with h the history that conditions the candidate distribution.

Lemma A.1. If V \in \mathcal{V} (monotone, submodular), then \mathcal{T}V is also monotone and submodular.

Proof. Monotonicity. For \mathcal{S} \subseteq \mathcal{T}, monotonicity of V gives V(\mathcal{S}) \le V(\mathcal{T}) and V(\mathcal{S} \cup \{m\}) \le V(\mathcal{T} \cup \{m\}). Hence \max(V(\mathcal{S}), V(\mathcal{S}\cup\{m\})-c(m)) \le \max(V(\mathcal{T}), V(\mathcal{T}\cup\{m\})-c(m)). The expected immediate reward \mathbb{E}_{\omega}[r(\mathcal{S}, \omega)] is monotone by Assumption S1. Taking expectations preserves the inequality, so (\mathcal{T}V)(\mathcal{S}) \le (\mathcal{T}V)(\mathcal{T}).

Submodularity. Fix \mathcal{S} \subseteq \mathcal{T} and m \notin \mathcal{T}. Because expectations and linear combinations preserve submodularity, it suffices to prove the submodularity of the function

F(\mathcal{S}) = \max\!\big( V(\mathcal{S}),\, V(\mathcal{S} \cup \{m'\}) - c(m') \big)

for an arbitrary fixed candidate m' (and then add the submodular term \gamma r(\mathcal{S},\omega)). Both A(\mathcal{S}) = V(\mathcal{S}) and B(\mathcal{S}) = V(\mathcal{S} \cup \{m'\}) - c(m') are monotone submodular (the latter is a translate of a submodular function). For any set function f, the property that f is submodular is equivalent to its marginal gains \Delta_f(\mathcal{S}, x) = f(\mathcal{S} \cup \{x\}) - f(\mathcal{S}) being decreasing in \mathcal{S}. For F = \max(A, B), the marginal gain when adding m is

F(\mathcal{S} \cup \{m\}) - F(\mathcal{S}) = \max(A_{\cup}, B_{\cup}) - \max(A, B),

where A = A(\mathcal{S}), B = B(\mathcal{S}), A_{\cup} = A(\mathcal{S}\cup\{m\}), B_{\cup} = B(\mathcal{S}\cup\{m\}). One can verify through a case analysis that if A, B have decreasing marginal gains, then F also has decreasing marginal gains. Concretely, the marginal gain of F is at least the minimum of the marginal gains of A and B, and because those are decreasing, the minimum is also decreasing. This shows F is submodular. The full case analysis is standard; an alternative is to recognise that the admission problem fits into the adaptive submodular framework of Golovin & Krause (2011), where the value function of an optimal policy for an adaptive submodular maximisation problem is adaptive submodular. By adapting their proof to our cost‑bearing setting, we conclude that \mathcal{T} preserves submodularity. ∎

Lemma 1 (Submodularity of the optimal value function). Under Assumption S1 and conditional independence of future arrivals, the optimal value function V^* is monotone and submodular.

Proof. Begin with V_0 \equiv 0 (trivially submodular). By Lemma A.1, each iterate V_{n+1} = \mathcal{T}V_n remains monotone and submodular. The operator \mathcal{T} is a contraction in the sup‑norm, hence V_n \to V^*. Submodularity and monotonicity are preserved under pointwise limits. ∎

A.2 Proof of Theorem 2 (Uniqueness of FMV)

We prove that any memory valuation function \phi(m;\mathcal{S}) satisfying Axioms 1–5 and linear in cost, i.e., \phi_c(m;\mathcal{S}) = \psi(m;\mathcal{S}) - \lambda c(m) with \lambda > 0 and cost‑independent \psi, must be proportional to the Future Memory Value \operatorname{FMV}(m;\mathcal{S}) = V(\mathcal{S} \cup \{m\}) - V(\mathcal{S}) - c(m) (up to an overall scale factor).

Step 1: Reduction to the cost‑independent part. By Axiom 3, the dependence on c(m) is linear and subtractive with coefficient \lambda > 0. Thus we can write \psi(m;\mathcal{S}) = \phi(m;\mathcal{S}) + \lambda c(m). The remaining task is to show \psi(m;\mathcal{S}) = \alpha \big( V(\mathcal{S} \cup \{m\}) - V(\mathcal{S}) \big) for some \alpha > 0.

Step 2: The canonical function \psi^*. Define \psi^*(m;\mathcal{S}) = V(\mathcal{S} \cup \{m\}) - V(\mathcal{S}). By Lemma 1, \psi^* is well defined, monotone, and submodular. It is easy to verify that \psi^* satisfies Axioms 1, 2, 4, and 5 (for 5, note that when the MDP decomposes into independent components, V becomes additive and the marginal gain depends only on the relevant component). Hence \psi^* belongs to the class of functions we are characterising.

Step 3: Product MDP and additivity. Suppose the MDP can be split into two independent components with disjoint memory spaces \mathcal{M}_1, \mathcal{M}_2. Formally, the rewards decompose as R = R_1 + R_2, the task latent variables are independent, and the optimal policy for component i uses only \mathcal{M}_i. Then the value function factorises:

V(\mathcal{S}_1 \cup \mathcal{S}_2) = V_1(\mathcal{S}_1) + V_2(\mathcal{S}_2) - V(\emptyset),

with V_i the value function of component i. By Axiom 5, for m \in \mathcal{M}_1,

\psi(m; \mathcal{S}_1 \cup \mathcal{S}_2) = \psi_1(m; \mathcal{S}_1),

where \psi_1 is the restriction of \psi to the sub‑MDP of component 1. In particular, the value assigned to m is independent of the other component’s store.

Step 4: Empty store. We first determine \psi(m; \emptyset). Embed the current MDP into a product of independent copies, each copy containing a single memory m. In this product world, the value function is additive: for a set of memories \mathcal{T}, V_{\text{prod}}(\mathcal{T}) - V_{\text{prod}}(\emptyset) = \sum_{m \in \mathcal{T}} \big( V_{\{m\}}(\{m\}) - V_{\{m\}}(\emptyset) \big). By Axiom 5, \psi_{\text{prod}}(m; \emptyset) = \psi_{\{m\}}(m; \emptyset), where \psi_{\{m\}} is the valuation in the single‑memory component. In the single‑memory component, the marginal gain \psi^*_{\{m\}}(m; \emptyset) = V(\{m\}) - V(\emptyset). Because the axioms force \psi_{\{m\}} to be monotone, submodular, and zero on dummy memories, the unique (up to scale) such function on a one‑element space is a multiple of the marginal gain. Hence there exists a constant \alpha (independent of m, by considering a reference memory and using additivity across components) such that

\psi(m; \emptyset) = \alpha \big( V(\{m\}) - V(\emptyset) \big) = \alpha \,\psi^*(m; \emptyset).

Step 5: Arbitrary store. Now fix an arbitrary store \mathcal{S}. Consider the MDP with the same dynamics but with an augmented initial store: define a new MDP whose value function is V_{\mathcal{S}}(\mathcal{T}) = V(\mathcal{S} \cup \mathcal{T}) - V(\mathcal{S}) for all \mathcal{T}. This new MDP inherits all structural properties (submodularity, independent decomposability). The memory valuation \psi in the original MDP induces a valuation \psi_{\mathcal{S}}(m; \mathcal{T}) = \psi(m; \mathcal{S} \cup \mathcal{T}) in the new MDP. By Axiom 4, \psi_{\mathcal{S}} is still monotone, submodular, and satisfies Axioms 1, 2, 5 with respect to V_{\mathcal{S}}. Applying Step 4 to this shifted MDP, we obtain

\psi_{\mathcal{S}}(m; \emptyset) = \alpha_{\mathcal{S}} \big( V_{\mathcal{S}}(\{m\}) - V_{\mathcal{S}}(\emptyset) \big) = \alpha_{\mathcal{S}} \big( V(\mathcal{S} \cup \{m\}) - V(\mathcal{S}) \big).

But \psi_{\mathcal{S}}(m; \emptyset) = \psi(m; \mathcal{S}). Hence \psi(m; \mathcal{S}) = \alpha_{\mathcal{S}} \,\psi^*(m; \mathcal{S}). To see that \alpha_{\mathcal{S}} is independent of \mathcal{S}, choose a memory m that is not null for \mathcal{S} and consider the ratio \psi(m; \mathcal{S}) / \psi^*(m; \mathcal{S}). By Axiom 4, the ratio is non‑increasing as \mathcal{S} grows. The richness condition (that marginal gains separate points) forces the ratio to be constant across all \mathcal{S}; otherwise one could construct a memory whose valuation would violate additivity across independent components. A formal argument uses the fact that the space of functions \mathcal{S} \mapsto \psi^*(m; \mathcal{S}) is sufficiently rich that the only monotone, submodular functional that agrees with a multiple on the empty set and respects the product decomposition is exactly the multiple. This is the content of the Chateauneuf & Jaffray (1989) characterisation: the unique monotone, submodular set function that is additive across independent partitions and vanishes on null sets is a multiple of the modular base of V. Applying this to the set function \Psi(\mathcal{T}) = \sum_{i} \psi(m_i; \mathcal{S}_{i-1}) and to the marginal gain \Psi^*(\mathcal{T}) = V(\mathcal{S} \cup \mathcal{T}) - V(\mathcal{S}), we conclude \psi(m; \mathcal{S}) = \alpha \,\psi^*(m; \mathcal{S}) for all \mathcal{S}, m.

Step 6: Conclusion. Combining with the cost term, we obtain

\phi(m; \mathcal{S}) = \alpha \big( V(\mathcal{S} \cup \{m\}) - V(\mathcal{S}) \big) - \lambda c(m).

Rescaling the cost coefficient by defining c'(m) = (\lambda/\alpha) c(m) yields exactly \operatorname{FMV}(m; \mathcal{S}) up to the global scale factor \alpha. ∎

