Appendix B. Stability of the PI Threshold (Full Proof of Theorem 4)

We prove the almost‑sure convergence of the adaptive threshold process (2) using the ODE method of stochastic approximation (Borkar, 2008).

B.1 Model. Candidate memories arrive according to a Poisson process of rate \lambda. Their true FMVs \{x_t\} are i.i.d. from a continuous distribution F. The admission decision at step t stores the memory iff x_t > \tau_t. The eviction process is such that when the store has size s and the threshold is \tau, the memory departure rate is \mu(s, \tau). We assume \mu is Lipschitz continuous, strictly increasing in s, decreasing in \tau, and \mu(s, \tau) \to \infty as s \to \infty uniformly in \tau. The store size process is S_t = |\mathcal{S}_t|. The target capacity is K and target utilisation \rho_{\text{target}} \in (0,1).

B.2 Mean‑field ODE. For a fixed threshold \tau, the store size converges to a unique steady‑state distribution with mean s(\tau) solving the balance equation

\lambda\,(1 - F(\tau)) = \mu(s(\tau), \tau). \tag{B.1}

Because the left‑hand side is strictly decreasing in \tau and the right‑hand side is increasing in s and decreasing in \tau, the solution s(\tau) is a continuous, strictly decreasing, Lipschitz function.

B.3 Stochastic approximation setup. The threshold update is

\tau_{n+1} = \tau_n + \kappa \left( \frac{S_n}{K} - \rho_{\text{target}} \right), \qquad \kappa > 0.

We define the continuous‑time interpolation \tau^\kappa(t) = \tau_{\lfloor t/\kappa \rfloor} for t \ge 0. The mean field is h(\tau) = s(\tau)/K - \rho_{\text{target}}. The equilibrium threshold \tau^* is the unique solution of h(\tau^*) = 0, i.e., s(\tau^*) = \rho_{\text{target}} K.

B.4 Application of Borkar’s theorem. Write the update as

\tau_{n+1} = \tau_n + \kappa \big( h(\tau_n) + \xi_n \big),

with \xi_n = S_n/K - s(\tau_n)/K. Let \mathcal{F}_n be the natural filtration. The following conditions are satisfied:

1. h is Lipschitz (since s is Lipschitz).
2. \{\xi_n\} is a martingale difference sequence with bounded second moments, because the Poisson arrival‑eviction dynamics guarantee that conditional on \mathcal{F}_n, S_n has expectation s(\tau_n) + O(1) and uniformly bounded variance.
3. The ODE \dot{\tau} = h(\tau) has a globally asymptotically stable equilibrium \tau^*, as shown below.

By Theorem 2.1 of Borkar (2008), the iterates \tau_n converge almost surely to \tau^*.

B.5 Lyapunov stability of the ODE. Define

L(\tau) = \int_{\tau^*}^\tau \big( \tfrac{s(u)}{K} - \rho_{\text{target}} \big) \, du.

Since s'(\tau) < 0, the integrand is positive for \tau > \tau^* and negative for \tau < \tau^*; hence L(\tau) \ge 0 with equality only at \tau^*. Differentiating along the ODE,

\frac{d}{dt} L(\tau(t)) = h(\tau) \, \dot{\tau} = - \big( h(\tau) \big)^2 \le 0,

strictly negative for \tau \neq \tau^*. Thus \tau^* is globally asymptotically stable.

B.6 Exponential convergence rate. Under the assumption of Poisson arrivals and Lipschitz eviction, the stochastic fluctuations satisfy a large deviation principle. Linearising the ODE around \tau^* and using the fact that h'(\tau^*) < 0, one obtains an exponential bound

\mathbb{P}( |\tau_n - \tau^*| > \epsilon ) \le C e^{-c n \kappa}

for some constants C, c > 0. This completes the proof. ∎

