Appendix C. Information‑Theoretic Bound (Proof of Lemma 2 and Corollary)

C.1 Proof of Lemma 2. Let p_0 = p(z \mid \mathcal{S}) and p_1 = p(z \mid \mathcal{S} \cup \{m\}). The task utility is

U(\mathcal{S}) = \mathbb{E}_{z \sim p_0}\!\big[ \max_{y} \mathbb{E}[R(y,z) \mid z] \big].

Denote f(z) = \max_y \mathbb{E}[R(y,z) \mid z], which takes values in [0, R_{\max}]. Then

U(\mathcal{S} \cup \{m\}) - U(\mathcal{S}) = \mathbb{E}_{z \sim p_1}[f(z)] - \mathbb{E}_{z \sim p_0}[f(z)] \le R_{\max} \|p_1 - p_0\|_{\mathrm{TV}}.

Pinsker’s inequality gives \|p_1 - p_0\|_{\mathrm{TV}} \le \sqrt{\frac12 D_{\mathrm{KL}}(p_1 \| p_0)}. Hence

U(\mathcal{S} \cup \{m\}) - U(\mathcal{S}) \le R_{\max} \sqrt{\frac12 D_{\mathrm{KL}}(p_1 \| p_0)}. \tag{C.1}

C.2 Discounted sum and mutual information. The value increment \Delta V_{\mathcal{S}}(m) = V(\mathcal{S} \cup \{m\}) - V(\mathcal{S}) is the discounted expectation of the future utility gains. Assuming each future task draws an independent latent variable Z_k whose posterior is influenced by m, we have

\Delta V_{\mathcal{S}}(m) \le \sum_{k=0}^\infty \gamma^k R_{\max} \sqrt{\frac12 D_{\mathrm{KL}}\big( p(z_k \mid \mathcal{S} \cup \{m\}, h_{k-1}) \,\|\, p(z_k \mid \mathcal{S}, h_{k-1}) \big)}.

By the chain rule of mutual information and the concavity of \sqrt{\cdot}, the sum of the square‑root KL terms is bounded by \sqrt{\frac12 I(m; Z_{0:\infty} \mid \mathcal{S})}. Therefore,

\Delta V_{\mathcal{S}}(m) \le \frac{R_{\max}}{1-\gamma} \sqrt{ \frac12 I(m; Z \mid \mathcal{S}) }. \tag{C.2}

C.3 Tightness. The bound is tight in linear‑quadratic systems. If R(y,z) = -\|y - z\|^2 and the posteriors are Gaussian with a small shift in mean, the KL divergence equals half the squared Mahalanobis distance, and the utility improvement is exactly half the trace of the precision matrix difference. In such cases, U(\mathcal{S} \cup \{m\}) - U(\mathcal{S}) \approx \frac12 R_{\max}^2 D_{\mathrm{KL}}, making the square‑root bound sharp up to constants. Consequently, conditional mutual information provides a principled surrogate for the true memory value in these regimes.

