Appendix D. Actor–Critic Convergence (Proof of Theorem 6)

We verify the assumptions of the two‑timescale actor–critic algorithm of Konda & Tsitsiklis (2003).

D.1 Parameterisation and learning rates. The admission policy is \pi_\theta(a \mid m, \mathcal{S}), differentiable in \theta. The critic uses linear function approximation \hat{Q}_{\omega}(\mathcal{S}, m, a) = \phi(\mathcal{S}, m, a)^\top \omega. The critic updates with step size \alpha_t, the actor with step size \beta_t, satisfying

\sum_{t=0}^\infty \alpha_t = \infty,\; \sum_{t=0}^\infty \alpha_t^2 < \infty,\qquad
\sum_{t=0}^\infty \beta_t = \infty,\; \sum_{t=0}^\infty \beta_t^2 < \infty,

and \beta_t / \alpha_t \to 0 (the critic moves on a faster timescale).

D.2 Ergodicity. Under the adaptive PI controller (Theorem 4), the store size remains in a bounded interval with probability one. Combined with the soft threshold policy, the joint process (\mathcal{S}_t, m_t, a_t) is a positive Harris recurrent Markov chain. Hence for any fixed policy \pi_\theta, there exists a unique stationary distribution d_\theta.

D.3 Compatible features. The policy gradient theorem gives \nabla_\theta J(\theta) = \mathbb{E}_{d_\theta} [ \nabla_\theta \log \pi_\theta(a \mid m, \mathcal{S}) \, Q^{\pi_\theta}(\mathcal{S}, m, a) ]. We assume that the features \phi are compatible, i.e., \nabla_\theta \log \pi_\theta(a \mid m, \mathcal{S}) lies in the column space of the feature matrix for all \theta. This condition is standard and can be satisfied, for example, by a softmax policy with a linear approximator.

D.4 Convergence. Under these conditions, the fast‑timescale critic tracks the true value function Q^{\pi_\theta} with bounded error, and the actor recursion approximates a gradient ascent on J(\theta). Theorem 2 of Konda & Tsitsiklis (2003) guarantees that \theta_t converges almost surely to a compact set of stationary points of J. Since the gradient vanishes only at stationary points, the limit is a local optimum. ∎